chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chapter 12 of the 3rd edition, Designing and Deploying Command Line Programs, is new in this edition and teaches how to run Python programs outside the code editor: the vocabulary of program, script, command, shell script, application, app and web app; the terminal, with the cd, pwd, dir and ls commands, running programs and opening files by name; the PATH environment variable, how Windows, macOS and Linux search it, how to add a Scripts folder to it, and the which and where commands; virtual environments made with python -m venv .venv, activated, deactivated and kept away from the system Python; pip run as python -m pip to install, list, upgrade, pin and uninstall packages from PyPI, and the automateboringstuff3 package; the built-in values __file__, sys.executable, sys.version, sys.version_info, os.name and sys.platform and the ModuleNotFoundError check; text-based program design - short command names, command line arguments in sys.argv with argparse left to its documentation, clipboard input and output with pyperclip, colourful text with Bext, clearing the terminal with os.system, sound with playsound3 and verbose and quiet modes; the Snowstorm animation; PyMsgBox's alert, confirm, prompt and password; deployment with a batch file on Windows, a .command file on macOS and a shell script with a .desktop entry on Ubuntu; the ccwd and Clipboard Recorder programs; PyInstaller's --onefile build; and it closes with a summary, 6 practice questions and one practice program." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's command line examples reproduce under Python 3.14.6: a script run as python3 yourScript.py hello world received ['yourScript.py', 'hello', 'world'] and with the two words in quotation marks ['yourScript.py', 'hello world']; __file__ was an absolute path even when the script was started by a relative name, which is what the chapter prints and what Python 3.9 made the rule; os.name was 'posix' and sys.platform 'linux'; list(sys.version_info) gave [3, 14, 6, 'final', 0], where the chapter's own printed list, [3, 13, 1 'final', 0], lacks the comma after the 1, a typing slip and not a different value." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P08 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "python -m venv .venv under Python 3.14.6 made a .venv folder with bin/activate, a pyvenv.cfg holding include-system-site-packages = false, and, new since Python 3.13, a .gitignore; python -m pip list in the fresh environment listed pip 26.1.2 alone, whereas the chapter's listing shows pip 23.0 and setuptools 65.5.0, which is what a Python before 3.12 installed, since Python 3.12 venv no longer pre-installs setuptools (gh-95299); inside the environment sys.prefix != sys.base_prefix was True and outside it False, the test the venv documentation gives for detecting an environment." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P04, chx:P09, chx:P11 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's Ubuntu deployment was executed: a shell script launcher named yourScript, starting with #!/usr/bin/env bash and sourcing ~/Scripts/.venv/bin/activate, was made executable with chmod u+x (its mode became -rwxr--r--), found by shutil.which in a folder on PATH and run by name, and the script ran inside the virtual environment; but the chapter's macOS and Linux launchers run python3 ~/Scripts/yourScript.py with nothing after it, so yourScript spam eggs reached the script with sys.argv holding only its own path, and adding '$@' to that line forwarded spam and eggs, which the Windows batch file does with %*. The online chapter also prints two options with an en dash in place of a hyphen: read ... -n1 –s in its launchers, which bash rejected with read: `–s': not a valid identifier and exit status 1, and python –m venv and python –m pip in its prose, which Python 3.14.6 took as a file name and refused (can't open file)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P06 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's ccwd.py and cliprec.py were run unchanged in subprocesses with a stand-in pyperclip module, because this machine has no clipboard and the real pyperclip 1.11.0 raised PyperclipException (could not find a copy/paste mechanism): ccwd copied ~/Scripts, and ccwd .. copied ~, while the shell that started it still printed ~/Scripts for pwd, which is the chapter's point that os.chdir() changes only the program's own working directory; cliprec printed each new clipboard text once, skipped repeats and ended quietly when KeyboardInterrupt was raised inside its try block; KeyboardInterrupt is not a subclass of Exception, so an except Exception clause would not have caught CTRL-C." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P03 .
chx:F6 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's third-party packages were installed from PyPI and inspected, not run against a display or speaker: Bext 0.1.1 offers fg, bg, clear, width, height, title, goto and get_key as printed, but its cursor functions are hide_cursor and show_cursor rather than the hide and show the chapter lists; PyMsgBox 2.0.1 offers alert, confirm, prompt and password; playsound3 3.3.2 offers playsound, as the chapter's prose says, but the automateboringstuff3 2024.10.1 package, which the chapter recommends for the book's exact versions, requires playsound==1.3.0, the older package with a different name." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01 .
chx:F7 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "PyInstaller 6.22.3, installed into a virtual environment under Python 3.14.6, built python -m PyInstaller --onefile hello.py for a one-line, 23-byte Hello, world program in about 15 seconds, left a build folder, a dist folder and a hello.spec file, and the one executable in dist was 12,950,120 bytes and printed Hello, world!, larger than the chapter's close to 8MB, which describes Windows; python -m pyinstaller in lower case failed with No module named pyinstaller, as the chapter warns, and the PyInstaller manual states that its output is specific to the operating system and the Python version it runs under." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P14 .
chx:F8 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Current Python and current systems add to the chapter: PEP 668 lets a distribution mark its system Python as externally managed, and on this Ubuntu 24.04 machine python3.12 -m pip install pyperclip stopped with error: externally-managed-environment, so the chapter's advice to keep packages out of the system Python is now enforced; the pip user guide recommends python -m pip for the same reason the chapter gives; argparse, which the chapter leaves to its documentation, declares arguments, converts types and writes usage errors, and in Python 3.14 colours its help text and can suggest a mistyped choice; shlex.split() splits a command line the way a POSIX shell does, and shutil.which() answers the question the which and where commands answer." ; sen0414:cites chx:P05, chx:P06, chx:P07, chx:P10, chx:P12, chx:P13 .
chx:F9 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Conclusion"@en ; sen0414:findingText "For an advanced course, chapter 12 is best taught as the path a typed command takes to a running program: the terminal searches PATH, a launcher script activates a virtual environment and starts the interpreter, the interpreter hands the words to sys.argv, and the program reports back through its output and its exit code; the chapter's outputs hold under 3.14.6 except the setuptools line of its pip list, its macOS and Linux launchers need '$@' to forward arguments, its en-dash options must be typed as hyphens, and Bext's cursor functions and the playsound pin differ from its text, while argparse, PEP 668, the sys.prefix test for an environment and the measured size of a PyInstaller build are what the course adds." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P04, chx:P05, chx:P12, chx:P14 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "23 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology." ; sen0414:citationStrength "14 sources, all opened and verified at 2026-10-11T12:00:00; 14 cited by findings." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
